Awase Dashi
  --Namiko Hirasawa Chen

1 piece kombu (dried kelp) (10 g; 4x4", 10x10 cm per piece)
1 c katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes) (packed; 10 g)
4 c water

Cold brew:
Put 4 c water and the kombu in a large bottle and let it steep on the counter for 2–3 hr in the summertime and 4–5 hr in the wintertime. You can also cold brew the kombu dashi overnight in the refrigerator.

Make several slits in 1 piece kombu (dried kelp) to release more flavor. Add the kombu and water to a medium saucepan. If you have cold brew Kombu Dashi, add the Kombu Dashi and hydrated kombu to the saucepan. Turn on the heat to medium low and slowly bring to almost boiling, ~10 min. Meanwhile, clean the dashi by skimming the foam and debris from the surface. Just before the dashi starts boiling gently, remove the kombu from the saucepan (discard or repurpose in other recipes—suggestions). If you leave the kombu in the saucepan, the dashi will become slimy and bitter. Add 1 c katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes) and bring it back to a boil again. Once the dashi is boiling, reduce the heat, simmer for just 30 s, and turn off the heat. Let the katsuobushi sink to the bottom, ~10 min. Strain the dashi through a fine-mesh sieve over a bowl or measuring cup. Reserve the katsuobushi and repurpose it. The Awase Dashi is ready to use. If you are not using the dashi right away, store the dashi in a bottle or mason jar and keep it in the refrigerator for 3–5 days or in the freezer for 2 weeks.


Niban Dashi:
In a medium saucepan, put 2–4c of water and previously used kombu and katsuobushi from making the first dashi. Bring it to a boil over medium-low heat. Remove the kombu just before the liquid comes to a boil, then lower the heat, and cook for 10 min, skimming occasionally. Add an additional 1/2 c (5 g) of fresh katsuobushi and turn off the heat. Let the katsuobushi sink to the bottom and strain the dashi through the fine-mesh sieve.
